.TH TERM-PANE-MARK 1 "2026-10-23" "tmux-tools" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
term-pane-mark \- set or clear a state mark on the caller's tmux pane
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B term-pane-mark
.BR set | clear
.BR pending | busy
.RB [ \-\-pane
.IR pane ]
.RB [ \-\-owner
.IR pid ]
.RB [ \-\-exit-status
.IR code ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B term-pane-mark
lets a program running inside a tmux pane publish its state as a pane user
option. It only sets and clears options; the tmux configuration that renders
them owns their presentation.
.TP
.B pending
Sets
.B @term_notify_pending
to
.BR 1 :
the program asked for attention.
.BR term-notify-sound (1)
sets it after ringing. The consuming configuration normally clears it when the
pane is visited.
.TP
.B busy
Sets
.B @term_busy
to
.BR 1 :
the program is working on a request, for example an agent between a submitted
prompt and its finished response. The program that set it clears it,
normally with
.B \-\-owner
so that a nested program cannot clear it early.
.PP
The caller's pane is
.B $TMUX_PANE
only while
.B TMUX
is set and
.B TERM_PROGRAM
is
.BR tmux .
A program launched from a tmux shell, such as an editor, inherits
.B TMUX
and
.B TMUX_PANE
but runs its own terminals, which replace
.BR TERM_PROGRAM ,
so a leaked pane id is never marked.
.PP
When
.B TMUX_PANE
is absent, as for agents that run hooks with the tmux variables removed, the
command looks up its controlling terminal, from
.I /proc
on Linux or with
.BR ps (1)
elsewhere, and uses the pane of the default tmux server whose pty is that terminal. Only a
process inside the pane can have it, so a terminal no pane owns, a missing
terminal, or an unusable
.B ps
marks nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-pane " pane"
Mark
.I pane
without the ownership lookup, for callers that already proved ownership,
such as a notifier that rang the pane's own pty.
.TP
.BI \-\-owner " pid"
Change the mark only while process
.I pid
runs in the foreground of the pane's terminal, that is, its process group is
the terminal's foreground group. A pane's agent is that foreground program.
Programs it launches, such as a reviewer or a memory classifier, inherit the
pane's tmux variables but run without its terminal, so with
.B \-\-owner
they can neither set nor clear the agent's mark, whatever order they start and
stop in. The terminal is read from
.I /proc
on Linux, where a missing entry means the process has exited, and from
.BR ps (1)
elsewhere.
.TP
.BI \-\-exit-status " code"
Attach a decimal shell exit status from 0 through 255 to
.BR "set pending" .
Options may appear in any order. Leading zeroes are accepted and removed.
A nonzero status sets the pane option
.B @term_notify_exit_status
before setting
.BR @term_notify_pending .
A zero status, or omitting this option, removes stale exit-status metadata
before marking the pane pending, so a later ordinary notification replaces
an earlier failure. Clearing pending removes both options. This option is
invalid with
.B busy
or
.BR clear .
The consuming tmux configuration decides how failures appear and when to
clear the pending mark and its status, normally when the pane is visited.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TERM_PANE_MARK_PROC_ROOT
Override the
.I /proc
root used to read terminal state, for tests.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The mark was updated or intentionally skipped: no owned pane, an
.B \-\-owner
outside the pane's foreground, no tmux, or a failed tmux update.
.TP
.B 64
The arguments were invalid. The command writes a usage diagnostic before any
tmux command is invoked.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tmux (1),
.BR term-notify-sound (1)
